      ":question/id": "f80e23ed-75a0-573e-afd5-23089725c8ca",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Simplify.\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{8}\\right)\\sec\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{16}\\right)\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Write $s=\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{16}\\right)$ and $c=\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{16}\\right)$. The **double-angle formula** gives $\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{8}\\right)=2sc$.\n\n The reciprocal definition gives $\\sec\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{16}\\right)=\\frac{1}{c}$.\n\nExpand the doubled sine and cancel the common factor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2sc\\frac{1}{c} &=\\frac{2sc}{c} \\\\ &=2s \\\\ &=2\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{16}\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll cancellations use nonzero factors in the original expression.",
